1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,687
In this video, you will follow Program One from beginning to end. We

2
00:00:04,687 --> 00:00:09,373
will teach a small SMS spam filter, save its trained pipeline, and load

3
00:00:09,373 --> 00:00:11,536
it to classify a new message.

4
00:00:13,050 --> 00:00:17,643
Start with messages you see every day. Can we meet for lunch is

5
00:00:17,643 --> 00:00:22,237
an ordinary message, called ham. Claim your free prize now is labeled
spam

6
00:00:22,237 --> 00:00:23,650
in our teaching examples.

7
00:00:25,200 --> 00:00:30,217
Our training data contains messages and their correct labels. The first
label belongs

8
00:00:30,217 --> 00:00:35,235
to the first message. Their order must match. Six examples explain the
flow,

9
00:00:35,235 --> 00:00:38,709
but are too few for a reliable spam filter.

10
00:00:40,250 --> 00:00:45,078
The pipeline has two steps. A text converter turns each complete message
into

11
00:00:45,078 --> 00:00:49,907
a list of numbers. The classifier uses that list to predict ham or

12
00:00:49,907 --> 00:00:50,278
spam.

13
00:00:51,800 --> 00:00:56,656
Here, each column describes a word feature. A row represents a whole
message.

14
00:00:56,656 --> 00:01:01,513
These decimal numbers are real T F I D F values from our

15
00:01:01,513 --> 00:01:04,501
small example. We show only selected word columns.

16
00:01:06,050 --> 00:01:11,405
Now we call fit with the training messages and labels. The text
converter

17
00:01:11,405 --> 00:01:16,761
learns its vocabulary and word statistics. The classifier learns
numerical weights from the

18
00:01:16,761 --> 00:01:20,881
labeled examples. Both fitted steps now belong to our pipeline.

19
00:01:22,400 --> 00:01:27,491
The trained pipeline currently lives in Python's memory. If that process
closes, these

20
00:01:27,491 --> 00:01:32,583
in memory objects disappear. To reuse the trained work later, we need a

21
00:01:32,583 --> 00:01:34,541
file that stays on disk.

22
00:01:36,050 --> 00:01:40,998
Joblib dump writes the whole fitted pipeline to a file. The file
contains

23
00:01:40,998 --> 00:01:45,946
both the trained text converter and the classifier. Saving does not
train again.

24
00:01:45,946 --> 00:01:48,229
It preserves what fit already learned.

25
00:01:49,750 --> 00:01:54,620
Later, joblib load reads the saved file and restores the fitted pipeline
into

26
00:01:54,620 --> 00:01:59,490
memory. Loading can happen in a new Python process. We now have a

27
00:01:59,490 --> 00:02:02,487
pipeline ready to use, without calling fit again.

28
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:08,779
Our new message is: Can we meet for lunch tomorrow? We give that

29
00:02:08,779 --> 00:02:13,558
message to the loaded pipeline. Its fitted text converter prepares the
numbers using

30
00:02:13,558 --> 00:02:15,396
the same vocabulary as training.

31
00:02:16,900 --> 00:02:21,633
The trained classifier reads those numbers and returns ham for this
example. That

32
00:02:21,633 --> 00:02:26,365
means it predicts an ordinary message. This is a model prediction, so it

33
00:02:26,365 --> 00:02:28,913
can still be wrong on other messages.

34
00:02:30,450 --> 00:02:36,217
Here is the complete journey. Labeled messages teach the pipeline. Dump
saves it.

35
00:02:36,217 --> 00:02:41,985
Load restores it. Predict uses it. Notice that training comes before
saving. Prediction

36
00:02:41,985 --> 00:02:43,760
uses the fitted pipeline.

37
00:02:45,300 --> 00:02:50,149
Pause here and discuss with your group. Which two fitted parts must we

38
00:02:50,149 --> 00:02:53,133
save together? Think about your answer before continuing.

39
00:02:59,150 --> 00:03:03,934
We save the fitted text converter and the trained classifier together.
The converter

40
00:03:03,934 --> 00:03:08,718
must preserve the meaning of each numerical position. The classifier
depends on that

41
00:03:08,718 --> 00:03:11,294
same meaning when it makes a prediction.

42
00:03:12,800 --> 00:03:17,946
You are ready to run Program One. Watch the printed prediction, then
change

43
00:03:17,946 --> 00:03:23,091
the new message and try again. Only load joblib files from trusted
sources.

44
00:03:23,091 --> 00:03:26,258
Use the larger lab dataset to evaluate performance.
